  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/9/94/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_19.png",
  "plot_summary": "It's the typical 3-pages of \"let's fill up space doing mundane stuff before getting to the meat of the story\" finally gets us to the last panel of page 3 where Reed lays out why he's gotten everyone together (including Alicia).&nbsp; Reed thinks he saw on an old Egyptian artifact signs that an ancient Pharaoh was healed of blindness.&nbsp; He wants to go back and see if there's hope for Alicia.\n\nThey head off to the now-abandoned castle of Doctor Doom to use his time machine that he used when they first met back in [LINK:1962:FF_5|FF #5].&nbsp; And sure enough, with Alicia running the controls, they soon find themselves back in ancient Egypt, where they are attacked and have to spend the first couple of pages of their arrival battling soldiers and chariots.\n\n<b>CONTINUE HERE</b>\n<b>CONTINUE HERE</b>\n<b>CONTINUE HERE</b>\n\nBut suddenly, all their powers seem to fade away, and before they know it, they're in front of the Pharaoh, a man named Rama-Tut, who not only can speak perfect English, actually <i>knows</i> who the Fantastic Four are!&nbsp; (<i>At least the Thing points out the English-speaking part in the text</i>.)&nbsp; Rama-Tut speaks as though the FF were something from ancient history!&nbsp; Turns out that he has the \"ultra-diode ray, invented in the year 3000\" - a thousand years farther in the future than even when the FF came from!&nbsp; He too is a time traveler!\n\nRama-Tut tells his story about being bored in the year 3000 with no adventures.&nbsp; Then he finds plans for a time machine \"while visiting the ruins of an amazing ancestor of mine.\"&nbsp; He re-creates the time machine in the form of an Egyptian idol and plans to become a time looter, with his headquarters being in ancient Egypt where he will be able to rule with his advanced technology.&nbsp; However, the time machine was damaged upon arrival in Egypt, and he injured his eyes, but the locals were able to obtain \"a rare herb\" that, in combination with the radiation from his damaged ship, healed his eyesight.\n\nNow he rules, and he aims his device at them, which saps their will, and demands that Sue become his queen.&nbsp; The others are aware of what they are doing but can't resist and serve at Rama-Tut's pleasure.&nbsp; But the Thing, now exposed to the sun for a long period of time, transforms temporarily back to Ben Grimm, who is now free of the effects of the ray.&nbsp; He makes his way back to the palace and surprises Rama-Tut long enough to throw the ray gun back to Sue, who releases Johnny from its effects.\n\nTut runs for his machine, and the FF pursue, but Tut is able to head out who knows where, leaving behind his Sphinx-shaped idol as an everlasting mystery to mankind.&nbsp; That leaves them to ponder whether they'll ever see him again, and Reed wonders who the \"ancient ancestor\" might be - could it be Doctor Doom himself?&nbsp; The Thing even wonders if Rama-Tut was actually Doom, who might have found a way to live for a thousand years and is the Doom of the future!&nbsp; Meanwhile, Sue finds a canister labeled \"Optic Nerve Restorative\" (<i>nice that it's printed in English, isn't it</i>?).\n\nThey have to flee though, because Rama-Tut set all his machinery to blow up, leaving no trace that his very existence.&nbsp; They are then retrieved by Doom's time platform and return to the 20<sup>th</sup> century, but what's this?&nbsp; The canister is gone!&nbsp; Reed was afraid of that - it contained radioactive material that Doom's machine would refuse to transport to and from the past.&nbsp; So their trip was a waste, but at least they know that such a substance exists, and Reed will continue to try and duplicate it in his lab.&nbsp; But Alicia is just happy they're back safe and sound.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: right | OldPharaohGainsSight | width=19%] The story's central objective is to go back in time in order to investigate how a blink Pharaoh was healed such that he could see, as Reed believes this might be just the breakthrough he needs to help Alicia regain her sight.\n[BR]\n[BR] This bit of continuity was good to see, since only three issues ago (in [LINK:1963:FF_16|FF #16]), Ben asked Reed to stop trying to change him back to human form and instead try and help Alicia, so I liked that he hadn't forgotten about his promise to try.\n\nNaturally, the standard set of questions arises, namely\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"How can there be a cure for blindness in the past, before the days of any atomic technology?\"]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Not to mention the <i>obvious</i> one of -- how do they get back to the past?)]\n\nThe answer to their time travelling question, at least, is to hope that Dr. Doom's time machine (that they used in [LINK:1962:FF_5|FF #5]) is still functional in his \"abandoned castle.\"&nbsp; And as fate (and Comic Book Convenience 101) would have it, the machine still works, and leaving Alicia behind to operate the controls (after \"hours of practice,\" according to page 5), off they go!\n\nThe book isn't even a quarter of the way over, and they're already transported to ancient Egypt!\n\nDuring requisite three pages of battle action that ensues when one just randomly appears next to the Sphinx, and the FF try to ward off an attack by the locals (which included chariots), their powers mysteriously fade away, and by the bottom of page 8, they're <i>defeated</i>!\n\n\n\n<b>CONTINUE HERE</b>\n<b>CONTINUE HERE</b>\n\n\n\nA solid upper-mid-grade offering with an interesting and novel twist in having the group go back in time to meet someone from the future, another time traveler.&nbsp; Little did Lee and Kirby suspect what a landmark story this would turn out to be.\n\nSpeaking with the benefit of hindsight on this tale, the \"amazing ancestor\" of Rama-Tuts is what is interesting and of course retconned later.&nbsp; And I give Lee and Kirby credit for the interesting speculation offered at the end of the issue about it possibly being due to Doctor Doom (we of course learn otherwise, but not until <i>years</i> later).&nbsp; This is, of course, the <i>actual</i> first appearance of Kang the Conqueror, though we don't learn that <i>either</i> until much later, and in fact, don't meet anyone going by the name of \"Kang\" until <i>Avengers</i> #8, almost a year later in real time (we'll get to that in the 1964 issues of course).\n\nThe book continues to regularly consume 2-3 pages of \"fluff\" material before finally getting to the matter at hand, and this issue was no exception, but I do take off points for that -- that might have been eliminated to allow more space to have explored Rama-Tut, but hey, it's the 1960s -- every origin or introduction tale was raced through at breakneck speed!\n\nAlso points off for Doom just leaving his castle's time machine lying around for anyone to find.&nbsp; <i>Really?</i>\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n\n\nAlso, you just have to ask:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=Who leaves a functional time machine just lying around for anyone to find?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(You'd think that such an important topic would be covered in the <i>Super-Villain Handbook</i>.)]",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * The word \"Pharaoh\" is once again misspelled \"Pharoah\" on the cover, in the title and throughout the magazine.&nbsp; Seeing as how I was originally reading a corrected reprint until I later discovered a scan of an original, I had missed this until I happened to notice the original cover and went back to check!\n[BR]\n[BR] For the record, this is the primary reason I now only read scans of <i>original</i> copies, providing I can find them."
  ],
  "letters_page": "I got the biggest kick out of Sidney D. Evans' short letter which reads:&nbsp; \"Dear Stan and Jack, I told my mother that you two read every letter you get whether it's printed or not.&nbsp; She said, and I quote 'I'll bet secretaries read and answer them!'\"&nbsp; with the response being:&nbsp; <i>We wish we HAD \"secretaries,\" Sid!&nbsp; We've got one overworked little gal who opens the letters for us, and then we read each and every one - honest!</i>  (This seems to be the first mention of the \"gal Friday\" who ultimately becomes known as \"Fabulous Flo\")\n\nEddie L. Malone's letter about his sister bashing him about buying \"a stupid mag named F.F.\" made me laugh when we learn he let his sister read it and ends with \"Now I don't have to buy F.F. - my sister beats me to it!\"&nbsp; (Naturally, the editor asks why he can't <i>also</i> buy one, heh.)\n\nMrs. Jo Shelby asks that the Thing be turned back to Ben Grimm permanently so that she doesn't have to keep explaining to her daughter what \"cosmic rays\" are when she inevitably asks every time \"Mommy, what's THAT?\"\n\nNone other than future Marvel contributor Steve Gerber (co-creator of Howard the Duck) writes in to provide his comments on both the good and bad of the FF, getting the response of:&nbsp; <i>Steve would be a great letter writer if he wasn't so shy about giving opinions!</i>",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}